![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `validatePromotionGate` function dereferences properties from `layers` and `inputDiagnostics` without defensive guards. If `layers` is nullish, or if `inputDiagnostics` is not an array, it will throw a TypeError. Using `Array.isArray` and optional chaining prevents these TypeErrors.

```javascript
function validatePromotionGate({
  promotionGate,
  layers,
  validatorInput,
  inputDiagnostics,
}) {
  const promoted = [
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.accepted) ? layers.accepted : []),
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.provisionalSafe) ? layers.provisionalSafe : []),
  ];
  const allLayerEntries = [
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.accepted) ? layers.accepted : []),
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.provisionalSafe) ? layers.provisionalSafe : []),
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.reviewRequired) ? layers.reviewRequired : []),
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.rejected) ? layers.rejected : []),
    ...(Array.isArray(layers?.blockedQuarantined) ? layers.blockedQuarantined : []),
  ];
  const criticalDiagnostics = (Array.isArray(inputDiagnostics) ? inputDiagnostics : [])
    .filter((diagnostic) => diagnostic?.severity === 'critical');
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When dereferencing and mapping properties from potentially missing or malformed input artifacts, use Array.isArray to safely default to an empty array if the property is not an array, preventing TypeErrors.
</details>

